What the recorded A32 machine actually contains
===============================================

Authority, snapshot, and exact incompleteness
---------------------------------------------
This machine description is intended for the original ARMv7-A A32
instruction bytes in the parent directory: genuine cross-compiled
objects for dqrls_, dqrdc2_, dqrsl_ from R's Fortran numerical path.
The authoritative architectural reference for interpreting those bytes
is ARM Architecture Reference Manual ARMv7-A/R, DDI 0406C.d. The
archived compiler selected -march=armv7-a, -marm, VFPv3-D16,
-mfloat-abi=softfp. Object format ELF32 little-endian ARM EABI v5.

This is NOT the existing English Major C 73-operation ARMv7 T32 grant.
T32 instructions can use two or four bytes; these are **A32** machine
instructions of four bytes each, with distinct encodings and their own
predicate conditions. Do not conflate A32 and T32 or assume the grammar
from the small summation program can execute QR VFP operations.

Program state to be declared before each instruction
---------------------------------------------------
1. Execution index: the memory byte address containing the next
   architectural instruction to fetch. For an ordinary permitted A32
   instruction completing normally with no branch, return, exception,
   instruction-set-state change, or write to PC, the successor is the
   old execution index plus exactly FOUR bytes. An object-file address
   such as 0x5c is relative to the unlinked section, not necessarily
   the final address in a process or a QEMU trace.

2. ARM-state PC operand: when an A32 instruction reads ordinary PC
   (R15) as an operand, the architectural value is usually the
   current instruction byte address plus EIGHT. That value is NOT
   identical to the execution index or the next sequential fetch
   address. Literal loads and PC-relative address computation depend
   on the instruction's exact encoding and PC-alignment rules.
   Branch-relative immediates use the architecture's PC+8 basis.
   Relocation entries can replace an object's placeholder branch
   displacement at link time.

3. R0 to R15 are 32-bit integer registers. R10 may be printed SL,
   R11 FP, R12 IP, R13 SP, R14 LR and R15 PC. Aliases identify the
   SAME physical machine registers, not additional registers.
   R14 (LR) often contains a return address after BL; it is not
   itself the execution index until an instruction branches to it.
   R13 holds the stack address. Multiplication can write two
   distinct register results. Operations with SP and PC must use
   their specific encoding/access rules, not generic assignment.

4. APSR condition flags: N (negative), Z (zero), C (carry), V
   (overflow). A32 operations can carry a 4-bit condition field.
   EQ/NE refer to Z; GT/GE/LT/LE inspect N/V with Z; HI/LS refer
   to C and Z. A failed ordinary predicate does NOT read/write
   the normal instruction's data operands or perform that memory
   access, though it consumes an instruction slot and advances
   the execution index. A relocated branch/call retains its
   condition meaning after linking.

5. FPSCR and VFPv3-D16: floating operations act on VFP D registers,
   normally holding a 64-bit double-precision payload in these
   routines. FPSCR tracks applicable floating-point status and
   control. VFP comparisons set FPSCR NZCV; VMRS can move these
   bits into the integer condition status before a later ARM branch.
   This distinguishes a VFP compare from an integer CMP; their
   condition effects and NaN/exception behavior cannot be guessed.
   VFP fused versus non-fused, rounding, denormal, signaling-NaN,
   signed-zero and saturation behavior require encoding-by-encoding
   ARM reference verification. A mnemonic family name alone is
   insufficient to claim a full numerical semantics.

6. Memory: byte-addressed, ELF32 little endian. LDR/STR normally
   load/store 4-byte words. LDRD/STRD transfer two adjacent 32-bit
   register words (eight bytes overall), subject to register-pair,
   addressing and alignment restrictions. Addressing may include
   immediate offset, pre/post-index updates and register shifts.
   LDM/STM and PUSH/POP transfer multiple integer registers with
   a definite address order and SP/base writeback; their aliases
   can hide this order. VLDR/VSTR, VLDM/VSTM and VPUSH/VPOP transfer
   VFP values, not ordinary integer register values. All data
   accesses can fault or be prohibited by memory permissions.

7. Procedure boundary: BL stores the return instruction address
   in LR and transfers to a target, possibly carrying a linker
   relocation to dqrdc2_/dqrsl_/BLAS/libc. A32 direct BL under
   ARM state sets LR to the address of the next A32 instruction,
   subject to link-time interworking stubs and ARM state changes.
   A successful return in these objects may be the POP restoring
   PC or a separate branch-to-LR form. Unlinked BL encodings
   printed with a zero placeholder are NOT calls to that literal
   self-address. Four entry argument registers R0-R3 plus stack
   arguments are used under AAPCS32; double/FP arguments and
   by-reference Fortran arrays follow the particular ABI and
   compiler output. Softfp is **not** hard-float register-argument
   calling convention. The recorded source routines use Fortran
   by-reference arguments, but mapping of each access requires
   tracing stack/register state rather than guessing from the
   Fortran routine signature alone.

Long operation names for instruction families in this capture
--------------------------------------------------------------
These names describe intended normal-case effects. Every single
record must still retain the original exact encoded machine word,
GNU mnemonic, operands and condition. Do not equate the names with
validated total state transformers.

- MOV / MOVW / MOVT: copy a 32-bit value, load the low immediate
  halfword while zeroing the remaining half, or replace the high
  immediate halfword while retaining the low half. A shifted
  operand can alter the data being copied. Without an 'S' setting
  condition flags, these do not modify APSR.
- MVN / BIC: complement every selected operand bit or clear the
  bit positions selected by the mask in a target register.
- ADD / SUB / RSB: add operands, subtract in normal order,
  or subtract in reversed operand order, all at 32-bit width
  with normal wraparound unless an explicitly signed/excursion
  rule applies. The S-bit determines whether APSR is written.
- CMP: compute arithmetic difference for NZCV comparison flags
  without writing its result. CMPLS is CMP executed only under
  the lower-or-same condition, not a separate mathematical family.
- LSL / ASR: shift bits left, or right while copying the sign bit.
  The shifter and register/immediate count encoding matter.
- MLA / MLS / SMULL: integer multiply-and-add; multiply-and-
  subtract; signed 32-by-32 producing separate low/high halves
  of the 64-bit product. Full operand order matters.
- LDR / STR / LDRD / STRD: read/write one or two adjacent 32-bit
  words, including the address-mode and register-pair effects.
- LDM/STM/PUSH/POP: transfer ordered groups of integer words
  between registers and memory, with base/stack address updates
  where the encoded form permits. POP including PC is a return
  or control transfer, not a normal sequential operation.
- B: set the next execution index to an encoded target if the
  predicate is satisfied; otherwise continue sequentially.
- BL: save the return address and branch if the predicate is
  satisfied, using the linker-resolved branch target. Does not
  prove the callee body is inside the recorded object.
- VLD*/VST*/VPUSH/VPOP: transfer one or multiple VFP register
  payloads to/from memory; address order and state must be checked.
- VMOV: transfer a VFP payload or individual bit pattern to
  the destination, with its encoded register width.
- VABS/VNEG: floating absolute value/sign negation, including
  special floating bit patterns under the architecture.
- VADD/VMUL/VDIV/VSQRT: floating add, multiply, divide or square
  root with FPSCR control and exception behavior.
- VMLS: VFP multiply-and-subtract result; do not silently call
  it mathematically exact or fused without checking the target
  VFP version's actual arithmetic steps and rounding.
- VCMP/VCMPE: floating comparisons, with their differing invalid
  operation signaling for unordered operands, writing FPSCR
  comparison condition state. VMRS copies FPSCR status to the
  targeted architecture register/status field.
- A suffix EQ, NE, GE, LT, GT, LE, HI or LS restricts the whole
  instruction's side effects to states satisfying the associated
  APSR predicate. Do not interpret 'VSTRNE' as a branch:
  it is a conditional memory store.

Literal pool and disassembly omissions
---------------------------------------
The original GNU objdump listings include literal-pool words shown
as ".word", often with R_ARM_REL32 relocations referring to read-only
numeric constants. These bytes are DATA in the current disassembly
and are NOT admitted as executable A32 instruction operations.
References from LDR [PC, offset] may read them. Their alignment,
placement and values matter to execution, even when ignored by a
naive list of instruction mnemonics.

The dqrdc2 listing contains a literal printed "..." by objdump
between instruction bytes and a literal pool. That indicates
omitted/unprinted bytes. A first-pass address-based concordance
must explicitly preserve a gap marker and not invent decoded
instructions in those omitted bytes. Reconstruct any missing bytes
only by inspecting the archived ELF section itself, not by guessing
that zero padding will remain zero in another compilation.

Competing implementations and why this path is used
----------------------------------------------------
A. Translate the genuine A32 machine code into long ordinary-English
   effects before invoking QR mathematics (CURRENT).
   Best provenance and faithful architecture-specific detail;
   requires lengthy ABI/FP/condition specification.
B. Start with Fortran formulas and fabricate convenient A32 steps.
   Shorter explanation but does not preserve the actual compiler's
   decision-making; REJECTED as a machine translation.
C. Treat x86-64 machine instructions as a one-to-one ARM equivalent.
   Tempting table but wrong for flags, vector state, calling ABI,
   instruction length and code generation; REJECTED.
D. Build a typed micro-operation interpreter with separate ARM and
   x86 frontends, then compare their state transitions.
   Promising for executable checking; DEFERRED until machine effects
   are properly defined.
E. Run equivalent numerical functions under emulation to compare
   externally observed coefficients and residuals.
   Useful system-level evidence but cannot alone establish
   instruction-by-instruction semantic equality; PLANNED and
   currently absent for QR.
F. Execute on a physical Android ARM32 CPU instead of QEMU.
   Required only for claims of concrete device ABI/driver behavior,
   not a prerequisite to source-level exploration; DEFERRED.

Open semantic blockers
----------------------
Before these English rows become an executable language, independently
audit each encoded instruction and predicate against DDI 0406C.d,
including exact register aliasing, carry/overflow and APSR liveness,
VFP FPSCR state, PC-relative literals, relocation/linker behavior,
stack argument mapping, memory alignment and faults, and external
numerical libraries. Without a complete linker/runtime image and
concrete input memory, the disassembly cannot produce an observed
full numerical execution trace. Preserve honest UNKNOWN boundaries.
